  pages={1-6},
  abstract={Electricity providers in Indonesia encounter the dual challenge of ensuring service quality while also maintaining a steady cash flow, as delayed payments pose serious financial risks. To tackle this problem, this research creates a hybrid Decision Support System (DSS) that merges data mining techniques with interpretable artificial intelligence to forecast and manage delays in customer payments. A significant innovation of this study is the inclusion of cluster labels produced by the DBSCAN algorithm as extra categorical variables for the XGBoost classifier. This integration improves the model’s capacity to identify underlying customer grouping trends, resulting in more precise and understandable predictions of default risk. Additionally, SHAP is utilized to offer both global feature significance and local interpretations, thus enhancing the interpretability of the prediction model. The suggested DSS framework integrates predictive modeling, clustering, and rule-based suggestions to assist utilities in prioritizing collection methods, providing customized payment solutions, and issuing advance notifications. Experimental findings indicate that the combined DBSCAN and XGBoost method yields reliable performance while delivering practical insights, presenting a valuable tool for proactive management of electricity-related debts. The scope of this study is confined to the creation and evaluation of a prototype model.},
  keywords={Decision support systems;Technological innovation;Electricity;Prototypes;Predictive models;Prediction algorithms;Market research;Delays;Data mining;Reliability;Hybrid;Data Mining;Decision Support System;Debt;DBSCAN;XGBoost;SHAP},
