  abstract={This research presents the development and assessment of a hybrid LSTM-ANN model designed to enhance cybersecurity in AI-based corporate environments. As AI technologies are increasingly integrated into business operations, the need for reliable and flexible cybersecurity solutions has become more critical. The proposed model merges Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks to capture temporal dependencies in sequential data with Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) to identify complex, high-dimensional patterns in cybersecurity datasets. Experimental results show that this hybrid approach achieves a 95 % detection rate with minimal false positives, outperforming individual LSTM and ANN models. The study uses datasets such as NSL-KDD and CICIDS2017 and evaluates performance metrics like accuracy, precision, recall, and F1score to gauge the model's effectiveness. The findings suggest that the hybrid model is highly efficient in detecting cyber threats, offering significant value to organizations using AI to secure their operations. While promising, the model's future research should focus on integrating explainable AI (XAI) techniques and expanding its application to areas like IoT and edge computing. This work offers a valuable contribution by proposing a strong cybersecurity solution tailored to the challenges of AI-driven environments.},
  keywords={Adaptation models;Accuracy;Explainable AI;Computational modeling;Threat assessment;Risk management;Reliability;Computer security;Long short term memory;Edge computing;Cybersecurity;Artificial Intelligence;Business Security;Risk Management;AI Ethics;Threat Detection},
